bbapprove doctor — diagnose the installation and the configuration.

Usage: bbapprove doctor [-v] [--offline] [--config FILE] [--disallow a,b]
                         [--token-name NAME]
       bbapprove doctor --help

Read-only: prints a report, changes nothing, never prints a secret, and needs no token,
forge access or config file. It also works when the configuration is broken: that is what it reports.

Options:
  -v              the full report: the default report plus the install root, the config lookup order and
                       file mode, where each disallow entry comes from, the environment and the tool paths
  --offline       skip the update check (nothing leaves this machine)
  --config FILE   diagnose this config file (BBAPPROVE_CONFIG does the same; the flag wins)
  --disallow a,b  add disallowed dependencies, as for a normal run
  --token-name NAME  1Password item that holds the token, as for a normal run (BBAPPROVE_TOKEN_NAME
                         and user_token_name do the same); only used when BBAPPROVE_TOKEN is unset
  -h, --help      show this help

Sections: one line each, in this order. A problem (warn or FAIL) is always printed, with what to do
about it, and the last line only counts them; no fact is printed twice.
  version      bbapprove version, platform, git checkout or release archive, the running binary
               (-v: and the install root); warn when the bbapprove on your PATH is another binary
  update       asks gh (or git in a checkout) for a newer release; skipped with --offline
  config       the file in use and how it was found (--config, BBAPPROVE_CONFIG, repo root,
               $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bbapprove/config.toml), that it is valid TOML and not still the
               template's example values; -v adds its mode and the state of all four candidates
               (used, not set, not found, shadowed). Then every effective setting with its source:
               [flag], [env], [file] or [default]; with -v the disallow
               row names where its entries come from. A forge adapter that is missing or not executable
               is reported under the forge type
  token        where the token comes from: BBAPPROVE_TOKEN (wins), else the 1Password item named by
               --token-name / BBAPPROVE_TOKEN_NAME / user_token_name; never a value, and doctor
               does not run op. FAIL when there is neither
  ignore       the per-user ignore file, its entry count and mode; an unreadable list is a problem
               (bbapprove then refuses to run); -v adds what the list does
  disallow     entries from BBAPPROVE_DISALLOW and the disallow file, when there are any (with -v
               they are part of the settings row instead)
  env          the BBAPPROVE_* variables that are set, plus NO_COLOR and FORCE_COLOR; -v prints
               values and adds XDG_CONFIG_HOME, EDITOR and VISUAL, but not what the settings show
  tools        git, gh and op on PATH (with their paths for -v), and whether gh is logged in

Markers: ok / ✓ fine, warn / ! worth a look, FAIL / ✗ a problem.
The symbols replace the words on a colour terminal.

Exit: 0 all good (warnings allowed), 1 a problem was found, 2 bad usage.

Colour only on a terminal; NO_COLOR turns it off, FORCE_COLOR=1 forces it on.
